.TH LT-QWWAD_FERMI_DISTRIBUTION "1" "September 2016" "lt-qwwad_fermi_distribution (Quantum Wells, Wires and Dots) 1.5.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lt-qwwad_fermi_distribution \- find Fermi-Dirac distribution functions for a set of subbands
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lt-qwwad_fermi_distribution
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Find the Fermi\-Dirac distribution functions for a set of subbands.
.SH OPTIONS

Generic options:
.TP
\fB\-h\fR [ \fB\-\-help\fR ]
display a help message
.TP
\fB\-v\fR [ \fB\-\-version\fR ]
display the version of the program
.HP
\fB\-c\fR [ \fB\-\-config\fR ] arg (=qwwad.cfg) name of the configuration file to be used
.SS "Program-specific options:"
.TP
\fB\-f\fR [ \fB\-\-fd\fR ]
Output Fermi\-Dirac distribution.
.TP
\fB\-m\fR [ \fB\-\-mass\fR ] arg (=0.067)
Effective mass (relative to free
electron)
.TP
\fB\-\-vcb\fR arg (=0)
Band\-edge potential [eV]
.TP
\fB\-\-alpha\fR arg (=0)
Non\-parabolicity parameter [eV^{\-1}]
.TP
\fB\-p\fR [ \fB\-\-particle\fR ] arg (=e)
ID of particle to be used: 'e', 'h' or
\&'l', for electrons, heavy holes or light
holes respectively.
.TP
\fB\-\-Te\fR arg (=300)
Carrier temperature [K].
.TP
\fB\-n\fR [ \fB\-\-nenergy\fR ] arg (=1000)
Number of energy samples to print out
.TP
\fB\-N\fR [ \fB\-\-global\-population\fR ] arg (=0) Use equilibrium population for the entire
system instead of reading subband
populations from file [x1e10 cm^{\-2}]
.SS "Configuration options:"
.TP
\fB\-V\fR [ \fB\-\-verbose\fR ]
display lots of information about calculation
.SH FILES
.SS Input files:
  'E*.r'    Energy of each state:
            Column 1: state index.
            Column 2: energy [meV].
  'N.r'     Population of each state (not used if --global-population is specified):
            Column 1: subband population [m^{-2}]

Default output files:
  'Ef.r'    Quasi-Fermi energy of each subband:
            Column 1: subband index.
            Column 2: energy [meV].
  'FDi.r'   Dispersion relation for subband i (if --fd option is used)
            Column 1: In-plane wave-vector [1/m]
            Column 2: Energy [meV]

In each case, the '*' is replaced by the particle ID and the 'i' is replaced by the number of the state.

It is assumed that all subbands have the same temperature. If the --global-population flag is used, the
subbands are assumed to be in thermal equilibrium with a single Fermi energy for the entire system.
.SH EXAMPLES
Compute the global Fermi energy for a system with total population 10e10 cm^{-2} and carrier temperature 20 K:
   qwwad_fermi_distribution --global-population 10 --Te 20

Compute the quasi-Fermi energies and carrier distributions using non-parabolic mass (alpha = 0.7 eV^{-1}):
   qwwad_fermi_distribution --fd --alpha 0.7
